from flask import Blueprint, Flask, current_app

main = Blueprint("main", __name__)

@main.get("/")
def index():
    return {"app": current_app.config["APP_NAME"], "testing": current_app.testing}

class FakeExtension:                       # the init_app pattern
    def init_app(self, app):
        app.extensions["fake"] = self
fake = FakeExtension()

def create_app(test_config=None):
    app = Flask(__name__)
    app.config.from_mapping(APP_NAME="Notes", SECRET_KEY="dev")
    if test_config:
        app.config.update(test_config)
    fake.init_app(app)
    app.register_blueprint(main)
    return app
prod, test = create_app(), create_app({"TESTING": True, "APP_NAME": "Notes-test"})
print(prod.test_client().get("/").json)   # {'app': 'Notes', 'testing': False}
print(test.test_client().get("/").json)   # {'app': 'Notes-test', 'testing': True}
print("fake" in test.extensions)          # True

concepts.body:
Flask apps are easy to test because the **test client**, `app.test_client()`, sends requests straight into the app with no network or server. With an application factory, the usual pytest setup is two fixtures:

```python
@pytest.fixture
def app():
    app = create_app({"TESTING": True, "SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI": "sqlite://"})
    yield app

@pytest.fixture
def client(app):
    return app.test_client()
```

`TESTING=True` lets exceptions reach the test instead of becoming 500 pages, so you see the real stack trace. (`sqlite://` with nothing after it is a fresh, empty database in memory.)

The client has one method per HTTP method: `client.get(url, query_string={...})`, `client.post(url, data={...})` for form fields, `json={...}` for JSON, `headers=...`, and `follow_redirects=True`. The response has `status_code`, `headers`, `text`, `data` (bytes) and `get_json()`.

The client keeps cookies like a browser, so the session works across calls: log in once, then request protected pages. `client.session_transaction()` lets you set session values directly, skipping the log-in steps.

Using `with client:` keeps the last request's data available after the call, so you can check `session` or `g` (a per-request scratch object, see the Contexts lesson) afterwards.

Code that needs the app but not a request, such as database helpers, runs inside `with app.app_context():`. Custom `flask` terminal commands are tested with `app.test_cli_runner().invoke(args=["init-db"])`.

Test behaviour through HTTP where it matters (status codes, redirects, JSON shape), and keep your app's rules in plain functions you can test without Flask at all.

